WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.610
Thanos and death are completely linked, but
the question of whether the Mad Titan might

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:08.880
actually meet his demise in Avengers: Endgame
isn't as clear as it may seem.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:13.679
There are plenty of reasons we could see his
nihilistic life come to an end in Endgame...but

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:16.280
there are also some good arguments that we
probably won't.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.110
The comics version of the snap that wipes
out half of all life doesn't happen at the

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:24.610
end of 1991's Infinity Gauntlet, or even close
to it.

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:27.400
It actually happens about halfway through
the first issue.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:31.380
That's partially because the story so much
of the movie Infinity War covers — with

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:36.120
Thanos acquiring the Infinity Gems — occurs
in the prequel comic Thanos Quest.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.840
It's also not the end of the story.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:42.080
After a massive battle in space that ends
with Thanos defeating most of the remaining

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:46.170
assembled superheroes from Earth, he has to
contend with the Marvel Universe's cosmic

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:47.359
heavy hitters.

00:00:47.359 --> 00:00:51.500
Eventually, he loses possession of the gauntlet
and it becomes something of a hyper-powerful

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:52.000
football.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.780
Nebula is the first to recover it, and Thanos
has to join forces with the revived superheroes

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:57.700
to get it away from her.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.100
Then Adam Warlock takes possession of it,
and he blasts Thanos into space, where the

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.420
Mad Titan seemingly explodes.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:09.460
What really happens is Thanos is sent 60 days
into the future, where he's living on an unnamed

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.770
planet and suffering from a fuzzy memory.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:15.900
He remembers his failures, though, and he
promises Warlock that he will lead a "quiet

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:16.840
life" as a farmer.

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:21.460
Infinity War doesn't perfectly mirror Infinity
Gauntlet's story, but it hits enough similar

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:23.840
beats that Endgame could recreate its ending.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:28.080
According to Marvel, one of Thanos's powers
is "immortality."

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.750
But he does dies one sort of death well before
Infinity Gauntlet even starts.

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:38.950
Marvel Two-in-One Annual #2 from 1977 is one
long fight between Thanos and a whole host

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:44.020
of Marvel superheroes, and it ends with Adam
Warlock using the power of the Soul Gem to

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:45.420
turn Thanos to stone.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:50.140
That story describes Thanos as "trapped within
the petrified form of his own corruption,"

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:55.939
but 13 years later, when Death revives him
in Silver Surfer #34, the cosmic nihilist

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:58.540
is described as having been full-on dead.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:02.880
The Marvel Comics embodiment of Death can't
have that, though, because she needs Thanos

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:04.200
to do her bidding.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:10.220
It's not until 2018's Thanos #18 that Death
finally decides to let The Mad Titan rest.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:14.630
Determined to prove his worth to Death, an
old Thanos transports his younger self to

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:19.080
the end of time for one big final showdown,
hoping to die in the process.

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:24.160
The old Thanos loses the fight, but his younger
self refuses to end his life.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.190
It's only after the younger Thanos goes back
to his time that Death declares his victory,

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:30.760
and allows the old Thanos to be erased from
existence.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:35.920
Comics stories keep going on and on forever,
and creators often want to keep characters

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:37.220
around for future tales.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:42.620
Movies have far more definitive endings, and
it's more common for characters to die permanently.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:47.420
Plus, superhero comics fit within their own
particular genre, while superhero movies tend

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:49.760
to follow a more standard action-movie template.

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:54.080
Ask any film fan what the defining action
movie of the last few decades is, and they'll

00:02:54.090 --> 00:03:00.150
probably say it's the 1988 classic Die Hard,
which ends with would-be terrorist Hans Gruber

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:01.200
falling to his death.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:04.960
It's no surprise, then, that the Joker dies
at the end of 1989's Batman.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:10.200
And not only does he die, he dies in exactly
the same way as Hans Gruber.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:14.060
That movie kicked off a trend in which the
main villains died in most of the subsequent

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:19.840
Batman films, The Rocketeer, X-Men 2, the
first two Blade movies, Spider-Man and Spider-Man

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:24.240
2, Hulk, Man of Steel, Deadpool, Logan — the
list goes on.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:25.400
"I won't kill you.

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.300
But I don't have to save you."

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:39.040
It stands to reason, then, that Thanos could
share the fate of all those films' bad guys,

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.620
particularly since Endgame is clearly aiming
for a grittier tone than some other MCU fare.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.940
Some of the earliest MCU villains absolutely
bit the big one.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:53.100
Iron Man's Obidiah Stane and Iron Man 2's
Ivan Vanko both died.

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:58.280
Malekith the Dark Elf didn't live to make
cookies another day after Thor: The Dark World.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:03.280
And thousands of members of the alien race
the Chitauri were nuked in Avengers.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:05.260
Ultron also seems to be pretty dead.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:08.420
At least as dead as an evil AI can be.

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:13.720
But more recent entries into the Marvel Cinematic
Universe have made conscious efforts to avoid

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:14.920
villain deaths.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:19.790
In Spider-Man: Homecoming, Peter Parker goes
out of his way to save the Vulture from imminent

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:21.600
death in the aftermath of a plane crash.

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:27.940
In Captain Marvel, Carol Danvers chooses to
send Yon-Rogg back to the Kree with a message

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:31.060
not to mess with Earth, rather than take her
revenge.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:33.520
Of course, there are exceptions to that rule,
too.

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:37.180
Hela doesn't make it out of Thor: Ragnarok
alive, for example.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:41.020
And Killmonger chooses to die rather than
go to jail in Black Panther.

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:44.340
Yet it's clear villains at least don't have
to die in MCU movies.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:50.320
In some very real ways, the stories of MCU
movies are still driven by the realities of

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.900
the movie industry, including actors' contracts.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:58.010
In the lead-up to Endgame, rumors have swirled
about the fates of Chris Evans' Captain America

00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:02.590
and Robert Downey Jr.'s Iron Man, among others,
because their contracts are seemingly coming

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:03.200
to an end.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:07.760
On the other side of things, Josh Brolin,
the actor who plays Thanos, may still have

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:10.020
a few movies left on his contract after Endgame.

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:15.220
ScreenRant categorizes him as "staying" on
as part of the MCU for a bit longer, noting

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:18.100
that he signed a "multi-picture contract"
with Marvel Studios.

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:23.260
Endgame is technically Brolin's fourth MCU
appearance, after cameos in Guardians of the

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:27.520
Galaxy and Avengers: Age of Ultron, and his
star turn in Infinity War.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:29.360
Was his contract for more movies than that?

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:31.200
Marvel hasn't revealed the details.

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:34.940
In an interview on Conan, Brolin did indicate
he's in for the long haul.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:36.680
"They're gonna tell me what I"m doing.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:39.020
I'll say, 'Yes, sir,' and then I'll leave.

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:41.240
And I get to play Thanos for, like, a few
years."

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:47.060
Rumors are often like dust in the wind, but
we'd say this one has maybe a 50/50 shot.

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:51.770
According to Total Film, word is swirling
that Brolin's Thanos will be appearing "in

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:55.810
some capacity" in the MCU Phase 4 film Eternals.

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:56.640
And it makes sense.

00:05:56.700 --> 00:06:01.960
Thanos' comic book brother, Starfox, is an
Eternal, a member of an ancient and powerful

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:05.030
band of early humans who left Earth and settled
in space.

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:07.100
Technically, Thanos is one, too.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:11.160
Like Starfox, he was one of the Eternals who
lived on Saturn's moon Titan.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:12.780
Naturally, there's a catch.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:17.000
If Eternals is an origin story similar to
what happens in the comics, then it will take

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:22.090
place thousands of years before the current
MCU — which means there would be no issue

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:24.820
with Thanos appearing, even if he dies in
Endgame.

00:06:24.820 --> 00:06:29.171
Unless, of course, there's also a modern-day
component to Eternals, like when the story

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:32.040
flashed forward at the end of Captain America:
The First Avenger.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:36.120
The MCU probably won't be rid of Thanos whether
he dies or not.

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:40.580
There's always the potential of him showing
up in a flashback or a prequel like Eternals

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:41.560
may turn out to be.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:46.780
The Red Skull returned in Infinity War some
75 years after his apparent death in Captain

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:48.200
America: The First Avenger.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:52.950
Instead of killing him, the Tesseract transports
him to the planet Vormir, where he becomes

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:55.540
a sort of cursed guardian of the Soul Stone.

00:06:55.540 --> 00:07:00.420
Arnim Zola, much like his comics counterpart,
kept his consciousness alive in a computer.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:02.260
And Loki has died how many times now?

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:05.520
"Sorry I tried to rule earth.

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:07.280
They’d be lucky to have you

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:12.100
Sorry about the thing with the Tessaract
I just couldn't help myself"

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:17.840
Endgame is a movie in which fans have an expectation
that dozens of characters will come back to

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:20.790
life after blinking out of existence in Infinity
War.

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:24.930
The MCU is officially enough of a comic book
world that death can be little more than a

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:27.060
stumbling block rather than a hard ending.

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:30.280
If Thanos does die in Endgame, will it even
matter?

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:34.020
Maybe the more poetic and fitting end for
a genocidal creep like Thanos isn't his own

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:38.710
death, which he doesn't seem to fear, but
to see his work undone, then have to live

00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:39.520
with his failure.

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:40.980
Just like in Infinity Gauntlet.

